    "plan_strategy": "Switch from the linear baseline to a gradient-boosted tree ensemble (hist_gbdt) fitted on all 12,967 training rows with early stopping disabled, so the fit sees every training row. House prices in this dataset are driven by strongly non-linear, interacting effects — lat/long/zipcode define location premia that no additive linear model can express, and sqft_living interacts with grade and location. A depth-limited but many-iteration boosted ensemble with a modest learning rate captures exactly that. Configuration: max_iter 600, learning_rate 0.06, max_leaf_nodes 63, max_depth 8, min_samples_leaf 20, l2_regularization 1.0, early_stopping false. Preprocessing: median impute (there is no missingness, so this is a no-op), no scaling since trees are scale-invariant, and no missingness columns (missing rate is 0.0 everywhere, so they would be constant and buy nothing). Judge on validation r2 against the 0.7718 bar and read train_val_gap: if the gap is large (>0.10) the next attempt lowers max_leaf_nodes / raises min_samples_leaf and l2; if validation r2 is still climbing with a small gap, the next attempt raises max_iter and leaf count."
